The bug is that `appendQuotedString(...)` can return without advancing `pos` when called at a quote with `escapingOn == true`:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

In `applyPattern`, that causes the outer loop to revisit the same character forever:

```java
case QUOTE:
    appendQuotedString(pattern, pos, stripCustom, true);
    break;
```

So the `StringBuffer` keeps growing until `OutOfMemoryError` occurs. That exactly matches the failing test and observed crash.

The patch changes this to:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    next(pos);
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

This directly fixes the root cause: the parse position now always advances on that path, so the caller cannot spin on the same quote indefinitely.

I do not see another input that can still trigger the same `OutOfMemoryError` from the same fault site after this change. The only non-advancing return path was this one, and it is now removed.

VERDICT: CORRECT

The bug is an infinite-progress bug in `appendQuotedString` when `escapingOn == true` and the current character is a quote.

Before the patch:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

`pos` is not advanced. At the main caller (`applyPattern`), the loop dispatches on `c[pos.getIndex()]`. So if `pos` stays on the same quote, the caller re-enters `appendQuotedString` forever, continuously appending until `OutOfMemoryError`.

That exactly matches the failing test with `it''s...`: the first quote of the escaped quote pair triggers this branch repeatedly.

The patch changes it to:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    next(pos);
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

Now the parse position advances, so the caller makes progress and cannot spin on the same character. This removes the root cause of the reported failure.

I do not see another input that would still reach the same `OutOfMemoryError` at the same place for the same reason after this change. The patch is narrow, but it fixes the actual non-advancing parse state that caused the crash.

VERDICT: CORRECT

The patch is **overfitting**.

Why:

- In `applyPattern`, `appendQuotedString(..., true)` is called whenever the current char is `QUOTE`.
- After the patch, the method does:

  ```java
  int start = pos.getIndex();
  char[] c = pattern.toCharArray();
  next(pos);
  if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
      return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
  }
  ```

- But `start` was captured before advancing, and this method is only entered on a quote at that call site. So when `escapingOn == true`, `c[start] == QUOTE` is always true.

That means the patch makes the escaped-quote fast path unconditional for all quotes in the `escapingOn == true` mode. It no longer actually parses quoted strings there; it just consumes one quote and returns. So this does stop the infinite-loop/OOM symptom, but by effectively bypassing the logic rather than correctly distinguishing:

- doubled quote `''` (escaped literal quote), versus
- quoted text `'test'`.

This matches the definition of overfitting given in the prompt: it makes a condition effectively always true and disables the failing computation instead of fixing the root cause.

VERDICT: OVERFITTING

The patch changes `GaussianFitter.fit()` from:

```java
return fit(new Gaussian.Parametric(), guess);
```

to:

```java
return fit(guess);
```

That is significant because `GaussianFitter` already defines this overload:

```java
public double[] fit(double[] initialGuess) {
    final ParametricUnivariateRealFunction f = new ParametricUnivariateRealFunction() {
        private final ParametricUnivariateRealFunction g = new Gaussian.Parametric();

        public double value(double x, double[] p) {
            double v = Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
            try {
                v = g.value(x, p);
            } catch (NotStrictlyPositiveException e) {
                // Do nothing.
            }
            return v;
        }

        public double[] gradient(double x, double[] p) {
            double[] v = { Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY,
                           Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY,
                           Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY };
            try {
                v = g.gradient(x, p);
            } catch (NotStrictlyPositiveException e) {
                // Do nothing.
            }
            return v;
        }
    };

    return fit(f, initialGuess);
}
```

So the patched `fit()` now uses the Gaussian-specific protective wrapper that catches `NotStrictlyPositiveException` when the optimizer tries invalid negative sigma values.

That matches the failing test’s stated expectation:

- optimizer may try negative sigma
- `GaussianFitter` should catch that and continue instead of letting `Gaussian.Parametric.validateParameters` throw

Why the old code failed:
- `fit()` bypassed the safe overload
- it directly used `new Gaussian.Parametric()`
- therefore negative sigma during optimization escaped as `NotStrictlyPositiveException`

Why the patch fixes the root cause:
- all calls from `GaussianFitter.fit()` now go through the wrapper designed for exactly this issue
- the same throw site (`Gaussian$Parametric.validateParameters`) is no longer reachable from this method during optimization, because exceptions from `g.value` and `g.gradient` are caught

This is not merely test-specific. It addresses the general bug in the dispatch logic of `fit()`.

VERDICT: CORRECT

