The original failure is caused by `appendQuotedString(...)` not advancing `pos` when it sees an escaped quote at the current position:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

At the `applyPattern` call site, that means control returns with `pos` unchanged, so the outer loop sees the same quote again and again, repeatedly appending until memory is exhausted. That exactly matches the observed `OutOfMemoryError`.

The patch changes that behavior to:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    pos.setIndex(pos.getIndex() + 1);
}
```

This removes the non-progress bug: after entering on a quote, the parser now advances and cannot get stuck on the same character forever.

I do not see a concrete input that still produces the same `OutOfMemoryError` at the same location after this change. The root cause of the reported failure was failure to advance `ParsePosition`, and the patch fixes that.

It is a bit awkward that the method no longer returns immediately from that branch, but awkward is not overfitting. It still ensures progress and avoids the infinite append loop.

